---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { RankTable } from '../../components/site/RankTable';
import { Faq } from '../../components/site/Faq';
import { LISTS, rankedList } from '../../lib/lists';
import { faqPage } from '../../lib/jsonld';
import { SITE } from '../../site';

const get = async (slug: string) => rankedList(LISTS.find((l) => l.slug === slug)!);
const makers = await get('ai-game-makers');
const noCode = await get('no-code-ai-game-makers');
const coding = await get('ai-coding-tools-for-game-dev');
const freeMakers = makers.filter((s) => s.tool.data.pricing.free_tier === 'yes');
const names = (xs: typeof makers, n = 3) => xs.slice(0, n).map((s) => s.tool.data.name).join(', ');

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can you make a game with AI for free?',
    a: `Yes. ${freeMakers.length} of the AI game makers we track have a free tier, including ${names(freeMakers)}. Free tiers usually limit exports, generations or commercial use.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to know how to code to make a game with AI?',
    a: `No. Prompt-to-game platforms such as ${names(noCode)} build a playable game from plain-language descriptions. Coding knowledge helps once you want more control, but it isn't required to start.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I sell a game I made with AI?',
    a: 'Often, yes, but it depends on each tool\'s license and plan. Many tools allow commercial use only on paid plans, and some open-weight models restrict commercial use. Check the commercial-use row on each tool page before you publish. Steam also asks you to disclose AI-generated content.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What kind of games can AI make today?',
    a: 'Small, focused games work best: arcade games, platformers, puzzle games, visual novels and simple multiplayer experiments. Large games still need a human to design the structure, while AI speeds up code, art and audio.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={`How to Make a Game with AI (${SITE.year} Guide)`}
  description="How to make a game with AI, step by step: choose between prompt-to-game platforms, AI inside a real engine, or a coding agent, then add AI art and audio and publish."
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/how-to-make-a-game-with-ai' }, { name: 'Make a game with AI', href: '/guides/how-to-make-a-game-with-ai' }]}
  jsonLd={[faqPage(faqs)]}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How to make a game with AI</h1>
    <div class="answer">
      <p>
        There are three ways to make a game with AI. A <strong>prompt-to-game platform</strong> such as
        {names(noCode)} builds a playable game from a description, with no code. An <strong>AI assistant inside a
        real engine</strong> (Unity, Godot, GDevelop) gives you more control and store exports. A <strong>coding
        agent</strong> such as {names(coding, 2)} writes the code for you in any engine. Beginners should start
        with the first option.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="meta">Updated {SITE.year} · Rankings below update automatically from our tool data.</p>

    <h2>Step 1: Keep the first game small</h2>
    <p>
      AI tools are best at small, focused games: one core mechanic, one or two levels, a clear win or lose condition.
      Write a one-paragraph description, for example "a 2D platformer where a fox collects acorns and avoids
      hedgehogs, with three levels." You'll reuse it as your first prompt.
    </p>

    <h2>Step 2: Choose your route</h2>
    <h3>Route A: Prompt-to-game platforms (no code)</h3>
    <p>
      You describe the game and the platform generates it, including code, basic art and hosting. It's the fastest
      route to something playable and usually runs in the browser. The trade-off is less control and fewer export
      options. Best for beginners, game jams and prototypes.
    </p>
    <h3>Route B: AI inside a game engine</h3>
    <p>
      Engines such as Unity, Godot and GDevelop now have AI assistants or agents built in, or available as plugins
      and MCP servers. You get a real project that can ship to PC, mobile and consoles, and AI does much of the
      setup and scripting. See <a href="/best/ai-for-unity">AI for Unity</a>, <a href="/best/ai-for-godot">AI for
      Godot</a> and <a href="/best/ai-for-unreal">AI for Unreal</a>.
    </p>
    <h3>Route C: A coding agent plus any engine</h3>
    <p>
      General coding agents can build a game from scratch, for example in Godot, Phaser or plain JavaScript. They
      also connect to engines through MCP servers. This route gives the most control and needs the most comfort with
      code. See <a href="/best/ai-coding-tools-for-game-dev">AI coding tools for game development</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Step 3: Generate the core loop, then iterate</h2>
    <p>
      Start with your one-paragraph description, play the result, and fix one thing per prompt ("make the jump
      higher", "add a score counter"). Small, specific requests work much better than rewriting the whole game.
    </p>

    <h2>Step 4: Add art and audio</h2>
    <p>
      Replace placeholder art with consistent assets. See <a href="/guides/make-game-art-with-ai">how to make game
      art with AI</a>, the <a href="/best/ai-sprite-generators">best sprite generators</a> and
      <a href="/best/ai-3d-model-generators">3D model generators</a>. For sound, see <a
      href="/best/ai-music-generators-for-games">music</a>, <a href="/best/ai-sound-effect-generators">sound
      effects</a> and <a href="/best/ai-voice-generators-for-games">voice</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Step 5: Check rights, then publish</h2>
    <p>
      Before you publish, confirm each tool you used allows commercial use on your plan. Our tool pages show this.
      Browser games can go on itch.io or your own site. For Steam you'll need a desktop build, and you'll have to
      disclose AI-generated content.
    </p>
  </article>

  <h2 class="mt-14 mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">The best AI game makers right now</h2>
  <RankTable ranked={makers} limit={8} />
  <p class="mt-3"><a class="text-brand text-sm font-medium hover:underline" href="/best/ai-game-makers">See the full ranking of {makers.length} AI game makers →</a></p>

  <Faq faqs={faqs} />
</Base>
